#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475c0859-7fcc-5659-abd8-58e1b393c37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316395} {:answer/id #uuid "293dbcb2-7a2d-5e82-9de9-5dbace7662f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316396} {:answer/id #uuid "96b35e10-146b-5f3e-aff3-06c6f77981b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316397} {:answer/id #uuid "669d5ef9-aaf0-5f35-b7b5-80d20c0be133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316398} {:answer/id #uuid "62e81304-d6ca-5bfb-88a4-14d4b95923a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b35e10-146b-5f3e-aff3-06c6f77981b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316397} :answer-field/id #uuid "c88c9f99-3dd9-520b-bbe3-2b38faec9e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225ad7f2-9d35-5125-9718-e80b689027c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103989" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x-4=A(x-4)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4)-4 & =A(4-4)+B(4) \\\\ 4 & =4B \\\\ 1 & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =A(0-4)+B(0) \\\\ -4 & =-4A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)} & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-4}{x(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c3d8055-eede-5c29-97fe-82f360115b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3d8055-eede-5c29-97fe-82f360115b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363995} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c1b3a98-215a-5022-815e-c7b8f7564029" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d985051-c488-50a2-aafb-4f3c91bfb279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d985051-c488-50a2-aafb-4f3c91bfb279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363997} :answer-field/id #uuid "45841253-2a32-59e9-b236-9dd537fd51bc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa3d60a4-4840-58c7-a2c5-661605cbc7a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-348896" :question/problem "Evaluate the following indefinite integral and fill in the missing coefficients.\n\n$\\int \\frac{7x+7}{(x+3)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-4).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7x+7}{(x+3)(x-4)} & =\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7x+7=A(x-4)+B(x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(4)+7 & =A(4-4)+B(4+3) \\\\ 35 & =7B \\\\ B & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-3)+7 & =A(-3-4)+B(-3+3) \\\\ -14 & =-7A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7x+7}{(x+3)(x-4)}=\\frac{2}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7x+7}{(x+3)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+5\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d29dfc73-399e-5999-a9d3-a2a5861985b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364000} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7a8659-cf41-5228-b5ca-730243762040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364001} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdd6bd5-ca2a-50da-b915-bc4e9da9b248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364002} {:answer/id #uuid "872a3d2b-76e5-5180-99df-ec8ed66227db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364003} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8fad18-3443-50e5-b9cd-1d7f93b1ee84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d8fad18-3443-50e5-b9cd-1d7f93b1ee84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364004} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b4b964-78de-5a62-aba8-77a905490a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17651bab-0045-573e-9f10-81a57a8387d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-49762" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4=(x-2)(x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{2x+6}{(x-2)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+6=A(x-2)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+6 & =A(2-2)+B(2+2) \\\\ 10 & =4B \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+6 & =A(-2-2)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 2 & =-4A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}=-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-\\frac{1}{3}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-{3}^{5}|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8e48a85-4ecb-5e56-9e25-99aecb4b14e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364007} {:answer/id #uuid "bcffeced-0ae9-5682-bba0-18502342e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364008} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0f473f-9419-5b16-8a6a-55e759a41868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364009} {:answer/id #uuid "45604210-a133-5820-8d1a-34550304d1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364010} {:answer/id #uuid "661d4cdf-9f36-585a-be75-bee3668d8561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "661d4cdf-9f36-585a-be75-bee3668d8561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364011} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a3d631-8d65-5484-b5f8-21ddd0f0659b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8143903c-337b-5c23-a61d-b90946f18ab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49765" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)} & =\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+8)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n11x+46=A(x+8)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-8,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-8)+46 & =A(-8+8)+B(-8+2) \\\\ -42 & =-6B \\\\ B & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-2)+46 & =A(-2+8)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 24 & =6A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)}+\\frac{7}{(x+8)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x+2}+\\frac{7}{x+8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{x+8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n            \n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{7})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4}) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29228550-4e97-5904-a7c7-467c5e70f2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316427} {:answer/id #uuid "05c4b379-dcf0-59a4-8582-f3ec5b5191a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316428} {:answer/id #uuid "ef95d2f7-960e-56a1-acdd-2683ff67d708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{8}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316429} {:answer/id #uuid "34a25c5f-0dde-5ad6-bec6-f91e14f37efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316430} {:answer/id #uuid "1291d401-1838-566b-a356-1b81bf63defc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1291d401-1838-566b-a356-1b81bf63defc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316431} :answer-field/id #uuid "38747f9e-0786-5d9e-90d4-91755e935282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf0cd4f5-d657-58c6-bc41-65da13b8c417" :question/math-academy-id "q-75828" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+16x+3=(5x+1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3} & =\\frac{14}{(5x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n14=A(x+3)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-3+3)+B(5(-3)+1) \\\\ 14 & =-14B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}+3)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{14}{5}A \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}=\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|{|}_{4}^{11} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
